Hitchhiking is legal in Iceland and, unusually in Europe, there is no motorway category to be shut out of: the ''umferðarlög'' has no ''hraðbraut'', and the Ring Road is an ordinary road the whole way round. [https://adverts.stjornartidindi.is/A_nr_77_2019.pdf Article 15 of the Traffic Act No. 77/2019] tells pedestrians to use the pavement or footpath, and permits the ''vegöxl'' — the shoulder — the cycle path or the carriageway itself where there is none or it is impassable. On the shoulder or carriageway you should as a rule walk on the '''left''' edge relative to your direction of travel, no more than two abreast, so that you face the oncoming traffic. That is exactly where a hitchhiker stands, and no provision makes asking for a lift an offence. Signs can still close a particular stretch or tunnel to pedestrians, so read them, and remember that the practical constraints here are weather and traffic volume rather than the law.
